content:
To handle the imagery for your application without running into copyright traps-while ensuring everything looks custom, high-end, and deeply aligned with the universal geometric framework-we should avoid using copyrighted commercial decks (like standard Rider-Waite-Smith art prints). Instead, we can build a Generative Vector & Symbolic Asset Engine right into your application.
Here is how we will approach thumbnails, comparisons, and visual assets so the app never feels plain:
1. The Visual Asset Strategy

Public Domain Vintage Base (The Rider-Waite Foundation): The classic 1909 Rider-Waite-Smith deck illustrations created by Pamela Colman Smith and Arthur Edward Waite are in the public domain globally, meaning the foundational line art can be safely utilized, adapted, and color-graded.
Procedural SVG & CSS Custom Borders (The Master Grid Look): Instead of flat raster images, the UI will dynamically render cards using lightweight SVG templates. Each card frame can incorporate sacred geometry, golden ratio borders, and celestial symbols tied directly to its Pythagorean key (3, 4, 7, 12).
ROYGBIV / Spectral Color-Grading: Rather than static colors, thumbnails dynamically shift their inner glow and ambient backdrop based on the card's position in the Trilateral Vector Grid (e.g., Major Arcana render in deep Indigo, Minor Decans in kinetic Yellow-Orange, and Court Cards in Emerald Green).

2. Implementing the Generative Card Thumbnail Component
You can integrate this lightweight JavaScript SVG generator into your client-side architecture (aroha_tarot_pipeline.js or directly inside index.html). It renders a custom, non-copyrighted structural card thumbnail complete with astrological coordinate badges and color-frequency backdrops:
// tarot_thumbnail_generator.js

export function renderTarotThumbnail(cardNode) {
  // Map arcana types to optical color frequencies
  const spectrumGlow = {
    'MAJOR': '#4b0082',      // Indigo
    'MINOR_DECAN': '#ff8c00', // Yellow-Orange
    'COURT': '#2e8b57'       // Emerald Green
  };

  const glowColor = spectrumGlow[cardNode.arcana_type] || '#ffffff';

  return `
    <div class="tarot-card-thumb" style="
      border: 2px solid ${glowColor};
      box-shadow: 0 0 15px ${glowColor}44;
      background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0b0b12 0%, #1a1a2e 100%);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 12px;
      width: 140px;
      height: 220px;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: space-between;
      color: #e5e5e5;
      font-family: monospace;
      position: relative;
    ">
      <div style="font-size: 10px; color: ${glowColor}; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px;">
        Key [${cardNode.number_key}]
      </div>

      <div style="text-align: center;">
        <svg width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="${glowColor}" stroke-width="1.5">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/>
          <path d="M12 3v18M3 12h18"/>
        </svg>
        <div style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 8px;">${cardNode.card_name}</div>
      </div>

      <div style="font-size: 9px; opacity: 0.8; border-top: 1px solid #333; pt-2;">
        <div>Ruler: ${cardNode.planetary_ruler}</div>
        <div>Sign: ${cardNode.zodiac_sign}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  `;
}

3. Visual Comparison & Preview Layouts
When users run a reading across the 4-Axis Trilateral Vector Grid, the comparison view displays side-by-side node comparisons:

The Grid Comparison View: Renders the Root Driver, Active Friction, Terminal Breakthrough, and Base Anchor cards horizontally. Each thumbnail pulses with its respective spectral light frequency.
The Structural Overlay: Hovering or clicking a thumbnail expands a comparative breakdown showing how its physical substrate (P) and planetary logic (L) intersect with the user's current baseline state.

This approach keeps the app visually striking, fully branded around your unique mathematical architecture, and legally robust without relying on restricted stock imagery.
